{
 "lines": [
  {
   "id": "L01",
   "text": "Marinara means sailor-style. Nobody knows for sure why.",
   "at": 0.25,
   "dur": 3.534
  },
  {
   "id": "L02",
   "text": "Tomatoes reached Europe from the Americas in the fifteen-hundreds. Some Italians fried them; plenty of others grew them for looks. They're cousins of deadly nightshade.",
   "at": 4.234,
   "dur": 7.296
  },
  {
   "id": "L03",
   "text": "In sixteen ninety-two, a Naples steward, Antonio Latini, printed one of the earliest known tomato sauce recipes: Spanish-style, for boiled meat, not pasta.",
   "at": 11.929,
   "dur": 8.628
  },
  {
   "id": "L04",
   "text": "The sailor stories? They come from tradition, not records.",
   "at": 21.007,
   "dur": 3.283
  },
  {
   "id": "L05",
   "text": "In Naples, marinara often means a pizza: tomato, garlic, oregano, oil. No cheese. In America, it mostly means the sauce.",
   "at": 24.64,
   "dur": 7.074
  },
  {
   "id": "L06",
   "text": "Recipe one: microwave marinara. Better than any jar.",
   "at": 32.214,
   "dur": 3.251
  },
  {
   "id": "L07",
   "text": "A fifteen-ounce can of tomato sauce. I like Cento. Two tablespoons of cheap red wine. Two or three teaspoons of Italian seasoning, rubbed between your palms. A glug of olive oil.",
   "at": 35.915,
   "dur": 9.857
  },
  {
   "id": "L08",
   "text": "Cover it loosely. Three to four minutes on high, stirring halfway.",
   "at": 46.171,
   "dur": 2.949
  },
  {
   "id": "L09",
   "text": "Then onion powder, and half as much garlic. No salt; the can's seasoned. Stir. Done.",
   "at": 49.47,
   "dur": 5.238
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "text": "Recipe two is even better, and there's still nothing to chop.",
   "at": 55.258,
   "dur": 2.821
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "text": "Oil in a skillet. Tube tomato paste; let it brown. Seasoning, thirty seconds. A splash of wine, maybe balsamic, and cook it down. Then the sauce, onion and garlic.",
   "at": 58.529,
   "dur": 9.625
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "text": "Now the best part. See the oil pooling at the edge? Sweep your spoon around it, swirl it into the middle, until tiny droplets catch the light.",
   "at": 68.654,
   "dur": 11.809
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "text": "Save this for pasta night. More recipes coming.",
   "at": 82.463,
   "dur": 2.218
  }
 ],
